\section{A Minimal Formal Core}

\subsection{Spaces and operations}

Let \(\mathcal{S}\) be a state space and \(\mathcal{R}\) a representation space.
Define
\[
E:\mathcal{S}\to\mathcal{R},
\qquad
D:\mathcal{R}\to\mathcal{S},
\qquad
T:\mathcal{S}\to\mathcal{S}.
\]
The composite transformation is
\[
F=T\circ D\circ E:\mathcal{S}\to\mathcal{S}.
\]
The recursive system is then
\[
S_{n+1}=F(S_n),
\qquad
S_n=F^n(S_0).
\]

\subsection{Basic properties}

\paragraph{Reversible encoding.}
An encoding is reversible on a domain if
\[
D(E(S))=S.
\]

\paragraph{Lossy encoding.}
If
\[
D(E(S))\neq S,
\]
then information has been discarded, compressed, or made inaccessible in the representation.

\paragraph{Invariant.}
A quantity \(I:\mathcal{S}\to V\) is invariant under \(F\) if
\[
I(F(S))=I(S).
\]

\paragraph{Fixed point.}
A state \(S^*\) is fixed if
\[
F(S^*)=S^*.
\]
In the framework this means
\[
T(D(E(S^*)))=S^*.
\]

\paragraph{Cycle.}
A state \(S\) lies on a cycle of period \(p\) if
\[
F^p(S)=S
\]
for some \(p\ge 1\).

\subsection{Minimal grammar}

A minimal expression language for the framework can be written as
\[
\begin{aligned}
\langle expr\rangle ::= {} & x
\mid E(\langle expr\rangle)
\mid D(\langle expr\rangle)
\mid T(\langle expr\rangle)\\
& \mid \langle expr\rangle \circ \langle expr\rangle
\mid F^n(\langle expr\rangle).
\end{aligned}
\]
Here \(x\) is an atomic state or representation, \(\circ\) is composition, and \(F^n\) is recursive iteration.

\subsection{Example: quadratic iteration}

To see how \(E,D,T\) can decompose a familiar recursion, take
\[
\mathcal{S}=\mathbb{C},
\qquad
\mathcal{R}=\mathbb{C}\times\mathbb{C}.
\]
Define
\[
E(z)=(z,1),
\qquad
D(z,1)=z,
\qquad
T(z,c)=z^2+c.
\]
Then
\[
F(z)=T(D(E(z)))=z^2+c.
\]
Thus the Mandelbrot iteration
\[
z_{n+1}=z_n^2+c
\]
is a special case of
\[
S_{n+1}=T(D(E(S_n))).
\]
